Compile a nested function at its first call, from the script's text.

QuickJS compiles every function when its script loads, and on Reddit
and GitHub fewer than a quarter of them are ever called. A function
nested in a script that keeps its text (not JS_EVAL_FLAG_NO_SOURCE) and
whose text is 128 bytes to 16 KB long (JS_SetLazyFunctions changes the
128; -1 turns this off) is now compiled at its first call.

Such a function is still parsed with its script, and it and everything
nested in it still go through resolve_variables, which is where QuickJS
finds the variables a function captures: each name a function cannot
find in itself marks the parent's variable captured and adds a closure
variable to every function between. The later passes are skipped, and
the function becomes a stub (js_create_lazy_stub): a JSFunctionBytecode
with no code, arguments or locals, holding its closure variables, its
flags, its text, and one constant, where its text starts in its line.
Everything nested in it is dropped.

OP_fclosure makes a function object from the stub as from any
bytecode. Its first call (js_lazy_link, from JS_CallInternal) parses
the stub's text again (js_lazy_compile) as a function with no parent
whose closure variables are the stub's, found by name as a direct eval
finds its caller's (JSFunctionDef.is_lazy in resolve_scope_var and
resolve_scope_private_field1), so the compiled function captures the
same variables in the same order. The compiled function replaces the
stub's constant, for the stub's other function objects. A private
class field's closure variable now keeps the field's kind
(resolve_scope_private_field1 gave JS_VAR_NORMAL), which the compile
reads back.

Kept compiled with the script:
- a function in code with a 'with', a direct eval or the variables a
  direct eval adds (js_fd_lazy_blocks), or that contains a direct eval;
- methods, getters, setters, class constructors and class static
  blocks, and export default function () {};
- a function expression that looks like it runs at once:
  (function () {})(), !function () {}(), x = function () {}(),
  (() => {})(), and, as in V8, any function expression after '(';
- a function expression in the script's own code (not in a function),
  mostly a module of a bundle, which runs while the page loads.

The bytecode writer writes a stub as one when the reader gets its text
too (JS_WRITE_OBJ_SOURCE_REF with a whole script's text, or the text
itself); otherwise it compiles the function and writes it compiled.
JS_WriteFunctionBytecode no longer fails on a function with no code
(js_malloc(0) is NULL).

Measured on the host against the same build compiling every function
with its script (2026-11-18): GitHub's repository page held 30.5 to
31.1 MB of script heap instead of 34.2 to 35.2 MB; Wikipedia's
"Macintosh" article 8.6 to 9.0 MB instead of 8.9 to 9.4 MB; Reddit,
whose loads differ from one to the next, 35 to 37 MB instead of 37.5 to
40 MB. A page that loads one 830 KB script into two frames and a worker
peaked at 10.1 MB instead of 14.6 MB (the worker at 2.7 MB instead of
4.3 MB).

